The Indus Valley Civilization (IVC), also known as the Harappan Civilization, was one of the earliest urban civilizations in the world. It developed around 2500 BCE in the northwestern parts of the Indian subcontinent, mainly along the Indus River and its tributaries. Important cities included Harappa, Mohenjo-daro, Dholavira, and Lothal.One of the most remarkable features of the civilization was its well-planned cities. Houses were built using standardized baked bricks, and many cities had straight roads, organized neighborhoods, wells, and advanced drainage systems. The Great Bath at Mohenjo-daro is an important example of their impressive engineering skills. Some cities also had large public structures and systems for storing and managing water.The people of the Indus Valley Civilization were involved in agriculture, trade, pottery, metalworking, and crafts. They cultivated crops such as wheat, barley, and cotton. They also traded with distant regions, including Mesopotamia. Archaeologists have found seals, beads, jewelry, pottery, and tools that show the skill of Harappan craftsmen.The civilization had a writing system, often found on seals and pottery, but the script has not yet been fully deciphered. Their religious practices are also not completely understood. Archaeological discoveries suggest that they may have respected nature, animals, and fertility-related symbols.Another interesting feature was their standardized system of weights and measurements, which would have helped them in trade and construction.After around 1900 BCE, many major urban settlements began to decline. Changes in climate, river patterns, environmental conditions, and trade may have contributed to this transformation. However, the people and cultural traditions did not simply disappear; many aspects continued in later communities.Overall, the Indus Valley Civilization was highly advanced for its time and made important contributions to urban planning, engineering, agriculture, crafts, trade, and early civilization in South Asia.

